<InfoPage title="Methodology: How Our Figures Are Made" heading="Methodology" slug="methodology" updated="2026-11-25"
  description="Where AtlasFigures data comes from, how live population counters are calculated, and how we label estimates and projections.">
  <p>This page explains where our numbers come from and how we turn them into the figures you see.</p>
  <h2>Where does the data come from?</h2>
  <p>Population figures come from the United Nations World Population Prospects 2024 (medium variant), the UN's official estimates and projections for every country. Where a national census gives a more detailed figure, such as a city's population, we use it and name it. A full list is on our <a href={path('/sources/')}>sources page</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="live-counters">How do the live counters work?</h2>
  <p>Each live counter starts from the UN's mid-year population estimate (1 July) and adds the UN's projected net change for that year, spread evenly across every second of the year. "Added today" and "This year" count from midnight and from 1 January in your own time zone. Live counters are estimates, not real-time counts of births and deaths, and every counter says so with the label "Estimate based on UN projections".</p>
  <h2>How are projections shown?</h2>
  <p>Any figure after the latest estimate year is a projection and is always labelled "UN projection". On charts, projections are drawn as a dashed line on a shaded band.</p>
  <h2>How are figures rounded?</h2>
  <p>Large numbers are rounded to one decimal place in millions in text (for example 177.8 million), while tables show the full figure from the source.</p>
  <h2>How often is the data updated?</h2>
  <p>We update pages when a source publishes new data. Each page shows when it was last updated.</p>
  <h2>Can I reuse the data?</h2>
  <p>Yes. Country pages offer a CSV download. Please credit "AtlasFigures" and the original source named on the page.</p>
</InfoPage>
